{
  "doc_id": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE:19843520-DS-8:discharge_sections",
  "register": "notes",
  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nAtrial fibrillation with RVR/Dypsnea\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ old male history of aortic stenosis status post \nbioprosthetic aVR, mitral regurgitation with flail leaflet \nstatus post mitral repair, CAD status post CABG, paroxysmal \natrial fibrillation, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and patent \nforamen ovale who was referred to the hospital from the office \nfor rapid\nventricular response and atrial fibrillation and worsening \ndyspnea.  \n\nHe was first noted to have atrial fibrillation sometime in ___. \nAt that time he was paroxysmal and not very symptomatic. He \nsubsequently presented in ___ with worsening shortness of \nbreath. He had a non-STEMI at that time.  Echocardiogram \ndemonstrated severe aortic stenosis along with severe mitral\nregurgitation which was new in the setting of flail leaflet.  \n\nHe started having episodes of atrial ablation with rapid \nventricular response and was transferred to the CCU where \namiodarone was started.  He also underwent an intra-aortic \nballoon insertion and coronary angiography. He was seen by\ncardiac surgery and underwent surgery on ___ \nconsisting of aortic valve replacement, mitral valve repair, \ncoronary artery bypass grafting ×3 with LIMA to the diagonal and \ndistal left anterior descending artery, and saphenous vein graft \nto the posterior left ventricular branch, and closure of atrial\nseptal defect. \n\nPostoperative course was notable for blood transfusion for acute \nblood loss anemia, and a platelet transfusion to correct \ncoagulopathy, rise in serum creatinine to 3 which subsequently \nstabilized, elevated lipase with unremarkable workup per the GI \nservice with subsequent increase in amylase lipase levels and \npatient asymptomatic. \n\nHe also had paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and was started on \namiodarone.  On amiodarone he converted to sinus rhythm but was \nbradycardic to the ___ so this was discontinued. He subsequently \nreverted to AF and he was discharged in atrial fibrillation on \nCoumadin.  \n\nPrior to the hospitalization he was actually on Xarelto. He \nstates that since being in rehab he has not been doing very \nwell. He still has significant dyspnea on exertion this is \nsimilar to what he had preoperatively. Compared to post \ndischarge status he is a little bit worse. He does have on and \noff palpitation throughout the last couple weeks while he has \nbeen in rehab. His\nedema has continued. He denies any significant PND but does have \northopnea.  He was treated with Lasix 20 mg daily at rehab. \nMetoprolol has also been titrated up to 100 mg 3 times daily by \nthe rehab physician. His INR on ___ was 1.8.  He denies any \nsyncope or near syncope. The sternal incision is healed well. \nHe denies any fevers or chills. There is been no GI symptoms \neither. All other ROS negative.\n\nIn the ED initial vitals were: \nTemp 98.4  HR 85 BP 112/74  RR 16  SaO2 100% RA \n \nExam notable for:  \nCV: irreg irreg rhythm  \nPulm: ___ crackles at lung bases  \nEXT: ___ pitting edema extending to knees \n  \nLabs notable for:  \n134 99 28\n----------<152\n4.6 21 1.2\n\nTrop 0.06  \n\n6.0>9.6/30.2<310\n\nUA bland\n\nImages notable for:  \nCXR ___\nInterval development of mild pulmonary edema with increased \nsmall bilateral pleural effusions, increased fluid tracking in \nthe right major fissure, and worsening opacification within the \nleft lung base, potentially atelectasis. \n\nEKG: atrial fibrillation with rate of 102bpm, likely LVH and \nnon-specific T waves changes.  \n\nPatient was given:  \n___ 14:41 IV Furosemide 40 mg \n\nOn the floor, patient confirms the story above and currently \ndenies SOB at rest, cough, CP, pain with urination, diarrhea. Is \nbothered by the swelling in his legs.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\n==================\nPRIMARY DIAGNOSES:\n==================\n\nAcute on chronic heart failure with borderline ejection fraction\nAtrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response\nSevere mitral regurgitation\n\n====================\nSECONDARY DIAGNOSES:\n====================\n\nHypertension\nCoronary artery disease\nGastroesophageal reflux disease\nGout\nHistory of HSV keratitis\nChronic hepatitis B",
